+
+\subsubsection*{New Method (experimental)}
+
+You can also change atom types using the LAMMPS' \verb|set atom| command. Specifically:
+\begin{list}{}
+\item - \textbf{Use ``set atom \$atom:ID type @atom:TYPE'' command.}
+\item - \textbf{Put this command in the} \verb|write(In Set Atom)| \textit{section. (See below.)}
+\end{list}
+
+In the following excerpt, three ethane molecules are created and cumulatively shifted along a vector. The third molecule is transformed from ethane to methanol by deleting two hydrogen atoms in the same methyl group, and then changing the atom types of all atoms in the selected molecule:
+
+\begin{verbatim}
+# Load the force field
+import "oplsaa2024.lt"  # defines the OPLSAA force-field (2024 version)
+
+# Define the molecule
+ethane inherits OPLSAA {
+  <<DETAILS OMITTED>>
+}
+
+# 1. Create the sample: 3 ethane molecules.
+mols = new ethane[3].move(5.0, 2.0, 2.0)
+
+# 2. We want to turn the 3rd molecule (mols[2]) into methanol, but
+#    methanol has two less atoms than ethane.  Delete the extra atoms.
+delete mols[2]/h22
+delete mols[2]/h23
+
+# 3. Now replace atom types to transform ethane in methanol.  (Note that all
+#    nearby atom types must be updated, not just "c2" and "h21".)
+write("In Set Atom") {  # ("In Set Atom" means new atom types->new FF params)
+  set atom $atom:mols[2]/c1  type @atom:OPLSAA/157  # C: CH3 alcohols
+  set atom $atom:mols[2]/h11 type @atom:OPLSAA/156  # H(C): methanol
+  set atom $atom:mols[2]/h12 type @atom:OPLSAA/156  # H(C): methanol
+  set atom $atom:mols[2]/h13 type @atom:OPLSAA/156  # H(C): methanol
+  set atom $atom:mols[2]/c2  type @atom:OPLSAA/154  # O: mono alcohols
+  set atom $atom:mols[2]/h21 type @atom:OPLSAA/155  # H(O): mono alcohols
+}
+
+# See "examples/force_field_OPLSAA/set_atom_type_example/" for details.
+\end{verbatim}
+
+
+\begin{warning}  
+   You must put the \verb|set atom| commands \textit{inside} the \verb|write("In Set Atom")| section \textit{if you want the force-field parameters to be updated}.  The \verb|write("In Set Atom")| is \underline{case sensitive}. The commands specified in this block will modify the atoms types and/or shapes in the DATA file.  \textit{(Those commands will also appear in the ``system.in.set.atom'' file.  Normally you can ignore this file.  You only need to import that file inside LAMMPS if you are changing other atom attributes besides type and shape.)}
+\end{warning}
+
+
+\begin{itemize}
+  \vitem \verb|set atom $atom:ID type @atom:TYPE-ID|\hskip2ex This command will change the atom type of a specific atom in the ``Atoms'' section of the DATA file.
+  \vitem \verb|set atom $atom:ID shape SIGX SIGY SIGZ|\hskip2ex This command will change the ellipsoid diameters of a specific atom in the ``Ellipsoids'' section of the DATA file, if the atom style includes ellipsoids.
+\end{itemize}
