-\subsection{Alchemical transformations}
-\label{sec:alchemical}
 
-In molecular modelling, an alchemical transformation involves transforming e.g. one atom type into a different one.
-LAMMPS already provides a \verb|fix alchemy| command, which is used for measuring thermodynamic differences between two different systems.
-This command is used to alter the composition of a system \textit{during} a molecular dynamic simulation, and relies on defining groups
-using the standard LAMMPS syntax.
-
-In moltemplate, alchemical transformations are used to change the type of individual atoms (and ultimately, the
-molecular species) and associated bonded interactions \textit{after} a sample has been created. These transformations can be seen as a
-post-processing modification of the ``Data Atoms.template'' file that is carried out right after executing \verb|ttree.py| in the moltemplate workflow,
-but before the force field lookup occurs.
-In this way, all \textit{bonded} force field terms are also updated, while preserving the atomic coordinates in the sample's reference system.\\
-%
-Alchemical transformations are triggered by a \verb|write_once("Postprocess")| command issued after the creation of molecules with the \verb|new| command.
-%
-Alchemical transformations are controlled using the LAMMPS' \verb|set atom| command. Currently, moltemplate can process the following keywords:
-\begin{itemize}
-	\vitem \verb|set atom ID type TYPE-ID|\hskip2ex This command will change the atom type of a specific atom in the ``Atoms'' section of the DATA file.
-	\vitem \verb|set atom ID shape SIGX SIGY SIGZ|\hskip2ex This command will change the ellipsoid diameters of a specific atom in the ``Ellipsoids'' section of the DATA file, if the atom style includes ellipsoids.
-\end{itemize}
-%
-The \verb|ID| is specified using moltemplate's internal identifiers, as in the ``Data Atoms.template'' file. Wildcard notation is supported for selecting atoms IDs.
-The \verb|TYPE-ID| is specified using the same variables in the force field definition, with the additional prefix if the ``inherits'' keyword was used to inherit attributes.
-
-These commands are parsed by the script alchemical.py: this tool is designed to be easily expanded, covering other properties or ways of selecting which elements to alter, e.g. molecular indexes.
-
